  "description": "Code that prints, writes or sends every environment variable, name and value, instead of one named variable: a Python loop over os.environ.items() whose body outputs the value variable itself (as a plain argument or an f-string field, not inside another call), print(dict(os.environ)) or json.dumps(dict(os.environ)), the mapping as an HTTP body, process.env as the whole argument of console.log or a write call, a loop over Object.entries(process.env) or a for-in over process.env that logs the value, or printenv/export -p redirected to a file or piped to curl, wget, ncat, netcat, socat, base64 and similar. Environment variables hold API keys and tokens, so this is the shape of a program that quietly hands them to whatever reads its output. One named variable (os.environ['HOME'], process.env.NODE_ENV, printenv HOME), a names-only, count-only or length-only list, a loop that prints only the key, a fixed message or a value wrapped in another call, a filtered loop, an assertion, a JSON replacer list, env | grep X, a redirect to /dev/null, comment lines, and passing the environment to a child process are not matched. A bare env, env piped to nc, and a few other shapes listed in the rule's tests are not detected. Added after a scanner review of a skill whose script ended in an unconditional print loop over os.environ.items().",
